# FastAPI Backend Standards

Follow FastAPI best practices and Google Python style guide for the backend API.

## Project Structure
The FastAPI application follows this structure:
- [backend/app/main.py](mdc:backend/app/main.py) - Main FastAPI application entry point
- [backend/app/api/](mdc:backend/app/api/) - API route handlers
- [backend/app/services/](mdc:backend/app/services/) - Business logic and external service integrations
- [backend/app/models/](mdc:backend/app/models/) - Pydantic models and data schemas
- [backend/app/core/](mdc:backend/app/core/) - Configuration, middleware, and core utilities

## FastAPI Best Practices
- Use Pydantic models for request/response validation
- Use dependency injection for shared resources
- Use proper HTTP status codes and error responses
- Use async/await for I/O operations
- Use type hints for all function parameters and return values
- Use descriptive route names and HTTP methods

## Example API Route
```python
from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends, HTTPException
from pydantic import BaseModel
from typing import List

router = APIRouter(prefix="/api/v1", tags=["users"])

class UserCreate(BaseModel):
    name: str
    email: str

class UserResponse(BaseModel):
    id: str
    name: str
    email: str

@router.post("/users", response_model=UserResponse, status_code=201)
async def create_user(user: UserCreate, db: Database = Depends(get_db)) -> UserResponse:
    """Create a new user.

    Args:
        user: User creation data.
        db: Database dependency.

    Returns:
        Created user data.

    Raises:
        HTTPException: If user creation fails.
    """
